    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Cast-iron tortilla press"
    },
    {
      "type": "image",
      "url": "https://i.guim.co.uk/img/media/3945c25a60949fcbc096c764e40e2bb4a9c7071e/0_0_1500_1500/1000.jpg?width=328&dpr=1&s=none&crop=none",
      "alt": "",
      "caption": "",
      "credit": "The Guardian · Culture"
    },
    {
      "type": "image",
      "url": "https://i.guim.co.uk/img/media/183d28fe0a85068e8520d461d0f861ebd2cd4da1/0_0_1284_856/master/1284.jpg?width=445&dpr=1&s=none&crop=none",
      "alt": "A cast iron tortilla press",
      "caption": "",
      "credit": "The Guardian · Culture"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "There’s something really special about a fresh, homemade tortilla. They taste better than anything you can get at the supermarket and are shockingly (at least I was shocked) simple to make. To make your masa (dough), just add water to your harina (maize flour) and roll into little balls. Though you can realistically flatten these balls with a rolling pin and some parchment papers, a tortilla press gives you consistent thickness. Plus, the bright red finish brightens up any kitchen. Danielle Renwick",
      "links": [
        {
          "text": "tortilla press",
          "url": "https://go.skimresources.com/?id=114047X1771840&url=https%3A%2F%2Fverveculture.com%2Fproducts%2Ftortilleria-kit-red-cast-iron&sref=https://www.theguardian.com/thefilter-us/2026/nov/11/best-international-gifts&xcust=abTestParticipations%7Cfronts-and-curation-editorial-test%3Ab"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "image",
      "url": "https://i.guim.co.uk/img/media/3945c25a60949fcbc096c764e40e2bb4a9c7071e/0_0_1500_1500/1000.jpg?width=328&dpr=1&s=none&crop=none",
      "alt": "",
      "caption": "",
      "credit": "The Guardian · Culture"
    },
    {
